    <DataNode elementId="CPD-18905" textLabel="chlorophyll a₂" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="122706303" dataSource="PubChem-compound" />
      <Graphics centerX="200" centerY="400" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="PMN, hawkins, SRI, caspi">The marine cyanobacteria of the |FRAME: TAX-1218| genus have evolved the production of divinyl chlorophylls as an adaptation to living deep in the water column. Divinyl chlorophylls are better suited to absorb blue light, which is the major wavelength found in deeper water. As of 2020, |FRAME: TAX-1218| is the only wild-type photooxytroph known to produce this pigment. |FRAME: CPD-18905 &quot;Chlorophyll &lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt;&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;&quot;| and |FRAME: CPD-22996| have Soret absorption maxima at 442 and 478 nm, respectively, which is red-shifted by 10 nm compared with with the maxima of |FRAME: CHLOROPHYLL-A| and |FRAME: CHLOROPHYLL-B| (432 and 468 nm, respectively). Some |FRAME: TAX-1218| isolates, notably SARG clones like |FRAME: TAX-167539| (SS120), have a Chl &lt;i&gt;b&lt;/i&gt;&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;/Chl &lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt;&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; ratio equal to or higher than 1, whereas other isolates display much lower ratios, with the |FRAME: TAX-59919| (MED4) strain exhibiting the lowest (0.13) |CITS: [10066832]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="CPD-18905" />
